{"id": "DfZkfIFR0Gt", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "Why does Shiva wear a snake, skulls, a boar's tusk and an elephant's skin? 🔱 Every one of them has a story in the Puranas.\n\nA follower asked us about Shiva's malas, so we went to the texts. Here's what each ornament is, and where it comes from:\n\n🐍 The king of serpents is his necklace: \"nāgendra-hārāya\" (Shiva Panchakshara Stotram)\n💙 The blue throat: the halahal poison from the churned ocean spread in all directions. Shiva drank it, and the blue mark it left \"is now accepted as an ornament of the Lord\" (Bhagavata Purana 8.7)\n🌙 The moon on his head: when he swallowed the halahal, the moon was made his ornament for relief from the poison, so he is called Shashibhushana (Skanda Purana, Prabhasa Khanda 1.18)\n🐗 The curved tusk: Varaha's, the tusk that killed Hiranyaksha. When it fell, Shiva took it up and wore it on his head and chest (Linga Purana 1.94)\n🐘 The elephant hide: the dying Gajasura asked Shiva to wear his skin. Shiva did, and became Krittivasa (Shiva Purana, Rudra Samhita)\n🌊 Ganga in his jata: the earth could not bear her fall from heaven, so he held her in his matted locks (Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda 42–43)\n💀 The skulls: of thousands of past Brahmas and Vishnus (Skanda Purana)\n📿 And rudraksha: grown from Shiva's own tears (Shiva Purana, Vidyeshvara Samhita 25)\n\nCrystal, karungali and the red thread are beautiful too, but they are what his devotees wear.\n\nOur picture books bring the stories of our gods to kids. The Navadurga Set is out now at rishikatha.com (link in bio).\n\n💾 Save this, and send it to a Shiva bhakt.\n💬 Which one did YOU not know? Tell us in the comments!\n\n#shiva #mahadev #shivbhakt #harharmahadev #neelkantha #bholenath #rudraksha #ganga #shivpuran #hindumythology #sanatandharma #hinduism #mahakal #omnamahshivaya #rishikatha", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1793966945, "like_count": 